        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        The best sunscreen for your face is the broad-spectrum, SPF 30 or higher formula that you will wear generously every morning, so texture, finish and tint matter as much as the number on the bottle.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Pick a broad-spectrum sunscreen with SPF 30 or higher and water resistance, in a texture you will happily wear every day, and apply enough: at least a teaspoon for the face, put on before you go out. Choose a tinted formula with iron oxide if you have dark marks, because it also covers visible light. Consistency matters most, since in a trial that ran for four and a half years, people who used sunscreen daily showed less skin ageing than people who used it only occasionally.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A face sunscreen has to do something other sunscreens do not: sit comfortably under moisturiser and makeup, on skin that you also care about looking good. That is why so many people buy one, dislike it and stop using it. The best formula is the one you actually wear.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What the label should say
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Broad-spectrum.</strong> This covers both UVA and UVB rays.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>SPF 30 or higher.</strong> This is the dermatologist minimum, and it should come with water resistance.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/sun-protection/shade-clothing-sunscreen/how-to-apply-sunscreen" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>A shade that blends in.</strong> Dermatologists recommend a formula that suits your skin tone, so it does not leave a white cast.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Iron oxide, for dark marks or melasma.</strong> Iron oxide protects against visible light, which causes dark spots, and it shows up in the inactive ingredients list.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Non-comedogenic, if your skin is oily.</strong> This means the formula is designed not to clog pores.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why a tint can matter
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Ultraviolet rays are not the only light that affects skin colour. Visible light can induce pigmentation, especially in darker skin tones, and broad-spectrum sunscreens protect against UV but do not adequately protect against visible light.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        To block visible light, a sunscreen has to be visible on the skin. Tinted sunscreens use iron oxides and pigmentary titanium dioxide to do that, and they come in many shades for all skin phototypes. Mineral filters made as nanoparticles to avoid a white cast do not protect against visible light.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> This is why tinted formulas are recommended for melasma and post-acne marks. To check a tinted sunscreen, look for iron oxide in the list of inactive ingredients, because only UV filters are listed as active ingredients.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
            The LNGVTY system
        </p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;color:#FFFFFF;font-size:31px;line-height:1.15;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.8px;">
            Choose by skin concern
        </h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;">
            If dark marks are the reason you are shopping for a tinted sunscreen, the serum below is designed to sit underneath it.
        </p>

        <a href="/products/dark-spots-pore-control-serum-30ml" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;width:100%;background:#FFFFFF;color:#181818;text-decoration:none;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2);">
